1
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:12,500
A 2009 Asus K50IJ whose disk controller hangs every Linux at boot. It now has no operating system.

2
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,500
At boot it pulls a 41 KB bare-metal kernel, written from scratch, over the network. Then the cable comes out, and it keeps living.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,000
Left: a worm in a petri dish. Right: its brain - 302 neurons, 10,193 synapses, from the public OpenWorm connectome.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Press H to poke its head: touch neurons ALM/AVM fire, activity runs along the wires to the command neurons, the motor area turns magenta - it reverses.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,500
Every line that lights up is a real synapse used in that step. Not an animation: a state the CPU computes 120 times a second.

6
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:40,000
Each body segment bends by its own dorsal and ventral muscles; the orange glow is food, and it climbs the concentration gradient.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,500
All of it runs on this 2009 dual-core CPU: int16 fixed point, SSE2 multiply-add, not one line of OS code.

8
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,500
A qualitative cartoon: the wiring is real, the neuron dynamics are a simplified integrate-and-fire model.

9
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:57,000
Chapter two. This face is not rendered by the dev PC: the camera only sends grey values, and the old machine's own neural network turns every cell into a character.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
A tiny MLP: 9 grey inputs, 32 hidden units, 16 glyphs, int16 fixed point, 1.8 KB of weights; 12,288 cells per frame inferred in ring-0 assembly, 10 frames per second.

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,500
The right panel is the model at work: the probe cell's input, hidden layer, output scores, weight maps and multiply-adds.

12
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:11,000
Bit-exact against the Python reference, cell for cell.

13
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,500
None of it has an operating system - no drivers, no runtime, not a line of C - all hand-written x86 assembly.

14
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,000
And so a scrapped laptop became bare-metal compute that can see, can think, and can be driven over the network.
